My proposal for how we build it

Rather than trying to write the entire Storage Specification in one pass, I suggest we treat it the same way we treated the MOS Core RFCs: as a sequence of focused specification documents.

The first document should become the foundation for everything that follows:

MOS Storage Specification (MSS) — Part I: Storage Foundation

It would define:

Storage Philosophy
Storage Axioms
Storage Domains
Storage Objects
Storage Classes
Storage Identity
Storage Lifecycle
Storage Guarantees
Compliance

Every subsequent chapter—transactions, indexes, PostgreSQL mappings, worker behavior—would build on those definitions without introducing conflicting concepts.

I think this is the right evolution of the project because we've kept a strict separation all the way through:

MOS Core RFCs define what cognition is.
MOS Storage Specification will define how cognition persists and evolves.
MOS API Specification will define how cognition is accessed.
MOS Worker Specification will define how cognition changes over time.

That separation of concerns is what will make MOS feel like a true operating substrate rather than a collection of database tables.
